#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: kr-mixed-concat-operands
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
also-codegen: v2
known-red: "js-v2 — `List(1) ++ \"x\"` answers `List(1)x` instead of `List(1, x)`. Real Scala (the JVM lane) agrees with the interpreter, so this lane is simply wrong. Remove when BUGS.md mixed-concat-operands-mean-three-things-on-three-lanes is fixed — the suite fails until you do."
---

# `++` with a string on the left and a non-string on the right

Executable record of BUGS.md `mixed-concat-operands-mean-three-things-on-three-lanes`, which
had no gate. The entry measured five cells; three agree across lanes and two do not, and the
two that do not are exactly "a string on the left, a non-string on the right".

The agreeing rows are included deliberately. Without them a lane that broke `"x" ++ "y"` or
list concatenation would still pass, and the case would be pinning the divergence while
missing a regression next to it.

TWO OF THE ENTRY'S FIVE CELLS ARE NOT LEGAL SCALA, and that is why they are absent here.
`"x" ++ 1` and `"x" ++ List(1)` are rejected by the JVM lane, which compiles real Scala:
*"None of the overloaded alternatives of method ++"*. So the entry's framing — three lanes,
three answers, pick one — is the wrong question: no answer is right, and every lane accepting
them is wrong to. Asserting any of the three values here would have frozen a behaviour the
language does not have.

What remains is legal, and on it the JVM lane is the ORACLE rather than a fourth opinion:
`List(1) ++ "x"` is `List(1, x)` in Scala, which is what the interpreter, v1 JS, JVM and V2 all
answer. The **js-v2** lane answers `List(1)x` — a divergence the entry does not record, because
its table covered the v1 emitter only.

```scalascript
def main(): Unit =
  println("x" ++ "y")
  println((1, 2) ++ (3, 4))
  println(List(1) ++ "x")

main()
```
